e on an Unbounded Interval" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157317 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157317 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "42f47d95-9f20-5488-98c4-cb80278a7cf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157494 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157494 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157494 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157494 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157494 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157494 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157494 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157494 #edb/ref 1045 "Modeling With the Geometric Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157494 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157494 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157494 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157494 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186157494 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4e86130c-1f86-5c43-8ce5-5c7ae15aaad2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157864 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157864 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157864 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157864 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\nI. The number of questions answered correctly on a quiz before the first incorrect answer\n\nII. The cost of an item randomly selected from a store\n\nIII. The number of products checked before finding a defective product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157864 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "24647bed-66c6-5672-aafa-5b790ade6e53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157864 #edb/ref 1074 "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Answering a question correctly (or incorrectly) is a binomial experiment, and the number of questions answered before the first incorrect answer can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. The cost of an item randomly selected from a store is not a binomial experiment.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Checking a product is a binomial experiment, and the number of products checked before the first defective product is found can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158161 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158161 #edb/ref 1061 27844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158161 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1fa58b97-b6b2-5edd-8734-614f560a7651" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158161 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158162 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158162 #edb/ref 1061 27845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158162 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "22e52493-867b-5374-829e-6bc8df9e2158" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158162 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158163 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158163 #edb/ref 1061 27846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158163 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7a7fc129-73fd-5136-a99a-5948c4181281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158163 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158164 #edb/ref 1061 27847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158164 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23f94e12-bb19-5644-a2f6-54b67243d945" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158164 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158168 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158168 #edb/ref 1061 27852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158168 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6ff378f4-047d-5e9f-ba7d-3a98770af4f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158168 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158171 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158171 #edb/ref 1061 27933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158171 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b13c477a-3272-5b6f-bce5-91e1831d0674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158171 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158492 #edb/ref 1064 "A **geometric distribution** is a probability distribution that models the total number of independent Bernoulli trials until the first success occurs.\n\nIf ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg) is the probability of success on each experiment, then the number of experiments needed before the first success has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/c6/c64756e38cf4152fdbcd3c3cb3c99e6368b7ec2549f6ea96e6badedeb7607cd8.svg)\n\nIf a random variable ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) follows a geometric distribution with probability ![](images/f4/f43942df7283a860efbcb722d0d28b46eca1b4d2ffc58a91a1858e36b0947101.svg) then we write ![](images/d1/d196f25e129c7b7bb4bdfff7f0383f5fa79d352b82ca3a0d8634fe7a64e0dc8c.svg)\n\nFor example, suppose we repeatedly flip a coin until we get heads. Each flip of the coin is a binomial experiment, and the probability of success is ![](images/f9/f98838fb4a565f05ea528dffcab8dad9e199e52bb3a2dfe071d6e11955e9ceb6.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) be the number of flips needed